# ESP32 IoT Controller

I created this project to make learning the basics of IoT development beginner-friendly. 🔌 Proxy support is an important part of this embedded system. Since I live in China, I have included proxy support in most projects so I can use Firebase's free tier. In some cases, I will also provide a proxyless version of the code.

> [!NOTE]
> ⚠️ Internal and external links may change at any time. Please be careful when using them.

## Tools

- 🛠️ VS Code with the PlatformIO plugin
- 🔥 Firebase Spark plan (free tier): https://firebase.google.com/pricing

## Project Files

📁 All project files are in the `archive` folder. I will try to keep the projects in sequence. To upload and test a project, copy the required code from `archive` to `src/main.cpp`. Below, I briefly explain each project.

## `archive/a_1_single_led_control`

💡 Controls a single LED using one-way control. A change on the server triggers a change in the ESP32 state; the ESP32 only reads from the server and does not write to it.

Using a proxy is not mandatory, but I needed one to communicate with the Firebase API from China. The proxy is a VPN running on my local PC.

### Setup

1. Create a project in Firebase: https://firebase.google.com/pricing
2. Create users in **Authentication**.
3. In **Realtime Database**, set the read and write rules using the user ID. Example:

   ```json
   {
     "rules": {
       ".read": "auth.uid === 'IRBYumRJO7W**************'",
       ".write": "auth.uid === 'IRBYumRJO7W**************'"
     }
   }
   ```

4. Import or create the database data. Example file: `archive/a_1_single_led_control/led-esp32.json`.
5. From **Your App**, copy the **SDK setup and configuration** values.
6. Update the `.cpp` code with your Wi-Fi and Firebase configuration.
7. Upload the code to the ESP32.
8. Change the value in **Realtime Database** and observe the ESP32. For example, `12:0` turns the LED off and `12:1` turns it on. 🔄💡

## `archive/a_2_multi_led`

This project uses the same one-way control method as the previous project, but controls three LEDs instead of one.

